\code{wjp_sankey()} draws a three-column flow diagram (a sankey) such as the
justice pathways of WJP country reports: a first column of nodes (e.g.,
contacted an adviser or not), a second column of nodes that each come from
one first-column node (their \code{parent}), and a third column of outcome nodes
that each second-column node flows into (its \code{outcome}). Every node carries
a boxed label with its printed value.
}
\details{
The function expects one row per node with its \code{column} (1, 2, or 3), its
key (\code{node}), its value (\code{target}, e.g. percentages that add up to 100 in
each of the first two columns), and its printed value (\code{value_labels}).
Second-column rows also name their \code{parent} (a first-column node) and their
\code{outcome} (a third-column node). Nodes are stacked from top to bottom in the
order of the data within each column.

Heights: first- and second-column nodes are as tall as their \code{target};
third-column nodes are as tall as the sum of the second-column nodes that
flow into them (their own \code{target} is not used for the geometry, so their
printed value can follow another definition). The tallest column fills
\code{flow_height} points including the gaps between its nodes.

Colors: flows, second-column bars, and third-column bars take the color of
their outcome (\code{colors}, named by the third-column node keys); first-column
bars use \code{node_color}. Flows from the first to the second column use
\code{alpha[1]} and flows into the third column \code{alpha[2]}.

Labels: \code{labels} gives the text of each box (use \code{"\\n"} for line breaks);
the printed value goes below it and the optional \code{notes} below the value.
Column titles are given by \code{headers}. The chart is drawn on a canvas of
\code{width} by \code{flow_top + flow_height + flow_bottom} points, so \code{\link[=wjp_save]{wjp_save()}}
writes it at that exact size.
